<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#norn-language">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Norn language</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Norn was the North Germanic language spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>, descended from the Old Norse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> settlers. It remained the everyday language of the islands for centuries after they passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1468 and 1469, but gave way to Scots and died out by the 18th century. Many of its words survive in local dialects and place names.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/norn-language.jpg" alt="Norn language" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caesar · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Languages_of_Scotland_1400_AD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
